co-founder Jonah I became really  fascinated by the idea of allowing  people to see the world through other  people's eyes right now and it was born  through I I had a trip to Istanbul in  late 2013 and the Taksim Square protests  were happening in gazi Park and I  remember thinking to myself he knows  it's safe for me to go there because I  was planning on traveling to Istanbul  but I really wanted to see was a a  picture or a live feed of what it looked  like on the street that my hotel was on  it and what it made us realize this  wouldn't it be amazing to essentially  like have this teleportation device that  would allow us to see what was happening  anywhere through someone's eyes and it's  had an amazing take off it's been very  popular but where do you see its future  it's been very popular with some of my  colleagues for example is it going to  replace broadcast news I don't I don't  see it as replacing broadcast news I see  it as as very much complementing  broadcast news some of the best  storytellers there are our  correspondents such as you know skies  skies very own mark stone and and these  folks are trained to tell stories in  very powerful ways and they  traditionally have done so through the  written medium on television but now  with mobile devices you can tell stories  and attract an audience through mobile  devices and get people where they are in  their pockets and so I think that's what  really compels me about the feature of  news and journalism through periscope  there's lots of other use cases that I  think make periscope charming whether  you're you know watching Roger Federer  walk on the grounds of Wimbledon  from his practice court to no centre  court as he showed us last week or  whether you're watching your nephews  first footsteps you know and have your  family and friends watching along that I  think is the power of periscope because  it takes you there and to say you know  stepping into someone's shoes and not  just experiencing it live but also  interacting with the broadcaster that  makes that's what makes periscope  different than television is the viewers  can say something and two seconds later  you know the question they ask gets  answered and that that interactivity is  what makes periscope special I think  have you got the right partnership and  platform paulus though because you know  you talk with interactivity there surely  you be better off with Facebook and  Twitter well no I mean I think about the  thing about Twitter is Twitter is the  the definitive source of live I think of  even before my company was acquired by  Twitter I mean when there's an  earthquake or  I hear a loud like I was in San  Francisco I remember very distinctly  four years ago I was in my old office  and I heard this loud bang it sounded  like an explosion and instinctively what  I did is I searched San Francisco  explosion and I think that's how you ask  a lot of people what Twitter is and  maybe they give you different answers  but that's the one thing people realize  about Twitter is it is the source of  life it is giving you a pulse of what's  happening around the world right now and  the funny thing is that's what periscope  is just through live video rather than  140 characters and that's why the  marriage between you know Twitter and  periscope I think makes a ton of sense  how do you keep it fresh because we've  seen you know post cope has had a rapid  ascent  you've left meerkat in the dust how do  you prevent periscope becoming the next  meerkat we have to innovate we have to  keep building things that people love to  use and that's why I think we're in a  really good place to do that because  we're so passionate about our vision of  what we want to do and also we like to  sort of philosophically be in a constant  state of disappointment around what  we've built because that's what  motivates us to keep making it better so  whether that's adding features or making  the features we already have better or  creating new discovery tools like we  added with we added a map feature that  the open periscope you can zoom in  anywhere in the world literally you can  zoom into Brazil and see all the  broadcaster lives a little red dots on  the map and you can click on them and  see what's happening right there that  that is a really powerful discovery tool  you know imagine the next time there are  protests in Baltimore there's an  earthquake in Nepal or a celebration in  San Francisco you can zoom in and  literally discover a place live so we  just need to keep innovating and adding  features like that and if we do I think  that's how we keep periscope fresh I  came back well it's been great talk to  you thank you so much for joining us and  sky news tonight
 

